WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Would you ever confess to a crime you didn't commit? Most people answer that question with a resounding no. Yet, according to the Innocence Project, almost 30% of wrongful conviction cases overturned by DNA testing involved a false confession. So why do people, seemingly of sound mind, implicate themselves? The answers may surprise you, but they shouldn't. In this episode, we speak with a psychologist about how law enforcement
你是否曾向一个自己并未犯下的罪行认罪？大多数人的回答都是坚决的“不”。然而，根据“无罪计划”（Innocence Project），通过DNA检测被平反的冤假错案中，近30%都包含虚假口供。那么，为什么看似神志清醒的人会自证其罪呢？答案可能会让你感到意外，但也理所当然。在本期节目中，我们邀请了一位心理学家，探讨执法人员

2
00:00:39.000 --> 00:01:17.000
current policies, and our own sense of justice can lead to false confessions. I'm Audrey Hamilton, and this is speaking of psychology. Saul Kasson is a distinguished professor of psychology at John Jay College of Criminal Justice. He has pioneered the scientific study of false confessions, and his research has been integral in preventing wrongful convictions and understanding why innocent people are targeted for interrogation, why they confess and the effect of this evidence on judges and juries. Welcome, Dr. Casson. Thank you.
当前的政策以及我们自身的正义感是如何导致虚假口供的。我是奥黛丽·汉密尔顿（Audrey Hamilton），这里是《心理学对话》（Speaking of Psychology）。索尔·卡森（Saul Kassin）是约翰杰伊刑事司法学院（John Jay College of Criminal Justice）的杰出心理学教授。他是虚假口供科学研究的先驱，其研究对于防止冤假错案、理解无辜者为何成为审讯目标、他们为何认罪以及此类证据对法官和陪审团的影响起到了不可或缺的作用。欢迎你，卡森博士。谢谢。

3
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Good to be here. Let's start right off the bat with the most obvious question. I think people have about false confessions. Why would anyone confess to something they didn't do? Do we understand the psychology of why this happens, especially in something like a murder investigation? Right. You know, it's interesting.
很高兴来到这里。我们直接从大家对虚假口供最常提出的、最明显的问题开始吧。为什么会有人承认自己没有做过的事？我们是否理解为什么会发生这种情况的心理机制，尤其是在谋杀案调查中？没错。你知道，这很有趣。

4
00:01:32.000 --> 00:02:08.000
That is the most interesting first question because when I ask people, would that be something you would ever do? The answer unanimously and strongly is no. nobody imagines they would ever do it short of having a gun to their head. The very short answer is that everybody has a breaking point, but it's a little bit more complex than that. It turns out there are three types of false confessions, and there are three different storylines as to why an individual would confess to something they didn't do. One is there is a category of false confessions known as voluntary false confessions.
这是最有趣的一个最初的问题，因为当我问别人“你会做这种事吗”时，大家的回答异口同声且非常坚决——“不会”。没有人会想象自己会做出这种事，除非被人拿枪指着头。最简短的回答是：每个人都有崩溃的临界点，但情况要稍微复杂一些。事实证明，虚假口供分为三类，对于个人为什么会承认自己未曾做过的事，也有三种不同的剧情。其中一类虚假口供被称为自愿虚假口供。

5
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:58.000
These are cases, and it often happens in high. profile cases that are in the news where people come out of the woodwork and volunteer confessions to crime the crimes that are in the news that they didn't commit. Kind of the poster child instance of that in history is when Charles Lindbergh's infant son was kidnapped in 1932, 200 people volunteered confessions and all of them were false. You see that again in high profile cases. Sometimes people volunteer confessions because they're seeking attention. Sometimes they're looking actually to protect somebody else who is the culprit. And sometimes it reflects some degree of delusion and it reflects on their mental health. Honestly, I don't see the voluntary
这些案件通常发生在新闻中备受瞩目的重大案件中，当时总会有人主动跑来，自愿为新闻中报道的他们并未犯下的罪行认罪。历史上最具代表性的例子就是在1932年查尔斯·林德伯格（Charles Lindbergh）的幼子被绑架时，有200人主动招供，而所有这些口供都是假的。你在高调的案件中总能再次看到这种现象。有时人们自愿认罪是因为他们在博取关注。有时他们实际上是为了保护真正的罪魁祸首。还有时，这在一定程度上反映了某种妄想，折射出他们的心理健康状况。老实说，我不认为自愿

6
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:31.000
false confessions while they happen and happen with some degree of regularity and always have. I don't see them as a particular problem for the criminal justice system. I think it's interesting that when somebody volunteers a confession to police, police typically react with some degree of skepticism. And they ask the question. And so they say, well, you say you committed this murder, prove it. What do you know about the crime? And if the individual who's offering to admit guilt can't also provide details about the crime
虚假口供——尽管它们确实会发生，并且具有一定规律性，过去也一直存在——是刑事司法系统的一个特大问题。我觉得很有意思的是，当有人主动向警方坦白时，警方通常会持一定程度的怀疑态度。他们会提出质疑。所以他们会说：“好吧，你说你犯了这起谋杀案，证明给我们看。关于这起犯罪你都知道些什么？”如果这个主动认罪的人无法提供与警方所掌握的、准确的案情细节

7
00:03:31.000 --> 00:04:02.000
that are accurate as known to the police, then the police don't follow that case. And so those voluntary false confessions don't tend to enter the criminal justice system as problematic. The problematic are the next two types. And these are the types of false confessions that arise from police interrogations. These are innocent people who, when asked about the crime, deny any involvement, and then they are subject to a process of interrogation. And it is as a result of that process of interrogation that a confession,
相符的信息，警方就不会推进该案。因此，这类自愿虚假口供往往不会作为严重问题进入刑事司法系统。真正成问题的是接下来这两种。这些是由警方审讯引发的虚假口供。这些都是无辜的人，当被问及罪行时，他们否认任何牵连，随后他们便经受了一轮审讯过程。正是由于这一审讯过程，才产生了一份口供，

8
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:38.000
that a confession is produced. So these are categories of police-induced false confessions. The way in which the two categories differ is the most common form is you bring a person in who denies involvement, who is now subject to a harrowing and relentless interrogation. Promises may be made, threats may be made, promises implied, threats implied, stress level is increased. They're isolated, they're away from anybody who, familiar and essentially, to make a long story short, everybody has a breaking point. And these are
一份虚假口供。因此，这些属于警方诱导的虚假口供类别。这两种类别的区别在于，最常见的一种形式是：你把一个否认涉案的人带进来，此人随后遭受了一场痛苦且毫不留情的审讯。可能会有许诺，也可能会有威胁；有明示的承诺，也有暗示的承诺；有明示的威胁，也有暗示的威胁；压力水平不断上升。他们被孤立，远离任何熟悉的人。简而言之，每个人都有崩溃的临界点。这些都是

9
00:04:38.000 --> 00:05:27.000
cases where individuals who are innocent, who know they're innocent, break down and confess in order to extract themselves from a very bad situation. They do what psychologists have known people to do in stressful decision-making situations, which is they maximize their opportunity to get themselves out of that situation. And as for the long-term consequence, well, I'll worry about that when the time comes. They're just not thinking clearly anymore. Classic, recent, historic, I think example, the Central Park Five. There was a case where five false confessions were produced in a single investigation. And every one of those 14, 15, and 16-year-old boys who confessed later said, I knew I didn't do it. I thought I was helping myself out by cooperating.
那些无辜的、深知自己清白的个人为了摆脱极其糟糕的处境而崩溃并招供的案件。他们做出了心理学上已知人们在压力重重的决策情境下会做的事，那就是最大化他们摆脱那种处境的机会。至于长期的后果，好吧，等时候到了我再去操心。他们的大脑已经无法清晰思考了。一个经典、近期且具历史意义的例子，我认为就是“中央公园五人组”（Central Park Five）。那是一个在单一调查中产生五份虚假口供的案件。每一个当时14、15和16岁、后来招供的男孩都说：“我知道我没干。我以为通过配合能帮自己脱身。”

10
00:05:27.000 --> 00:06:13.000
And so that is, I think, the most common type of false confession. innocent person, broken down, and the metric of the fact that these are what we call compliant false confessions. That is, they confess as an act of compliance while privately remaining knowledgeable that they're innocent. The metric of that is as soon as the pressure of the situation is lifted, they recant the confession. But it's too late. It doesn't matter. Nobody pays attention to the recant. Nobody trusts the recantation as much as they do the confession. The third type of false confession is in some ways, when I first discover this, we wrote about this, Larry Reitzman and I wrote about this in 1985, was the most vexing because there wasn't a psychology in place yet to explain it.
所以，我认为这是最常见的一种虚假口供：无辜的人精神崩溃，而这种现象的衡量标准就是我们所说的顺从型虚假口供。也就是说，他们招供只是一种顺从的行为，但在私下里仍然坚信自己是无辜的。这种现象的衡量标准在于，一旦情况带来的压力解除，他们就会翻供。但为时已晚。这已经不重要了。没有人会去注意翻供。比起口供，没有人会相信翻供。第三种虚假口供在某些方面——当我最初发现这一点时，我和拉里·赖茨曼（Larry Wrightsman）在1985年撰文探讨过——是最令人头疼的，因为当时还没有相应的心理学理论来解释它。

11
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:48.000
We called it internalized false confessions. These are instances where an innocent person denying any involvement is put through a series of interrogation tactics and ultimately comes out not only willing to sign a confession as an act of compliance, but they come to internalize the belief in their own guilt. They come to believe they have committed this crime. Now that is a different type of process and a different story than what happens in the compliant false confessions. But what happens in internalization is this.
我们称之为内化型虚假口供。这些情况是指，一个否认任何涉案行为的无辜之人经历了一系列审讯战术，最终不仅愿意作为一种顺从行为签署认罪书，而且开始内化对自己有罪的信念。他们开始相信自己确实犯下了罪行。这与顺从型虚假口供发生的过程和故事截然不同。而内化过程中发生的事情是这样的。

12
00:06:48.000 --> 00:07:20.000
What's fascinating about them, I've seen about a dozen of them firsthand, where we can actually watch the process. And what's fascinating is the names change, the dates change, the places change. the place has changed. The script is almost always about the same. And the script is this. A suspect is brought in who is vulnerable to manipulation. And when I say vulnerable, I mean either they are vulnerable dispositionally. They may be mentally, intellectually impaired. They may be young.
最迷人的是，我亲眼目睹过大约十起这样的案件，我们能够亲眼目睹这个过程。迷人之处在于：名字变了，日期变了，地点变了。地点变了，剧本却几乎总是雷同的。剧本是这样的：一个容易受到操纵的嫌疑人被带了进来。当我说的“易受操纵”时，我的意思是他们可能在性格上就很脆弱。他们可能存在心理或智力上的缺陷。他们可能年纪很小。

13
00:07:20.000 --> 00:08:11.000
They may be 12, 13, 14, 16 years old. They may have been deprived of sleep and therefore rendered vulnerable to manipulation, they may be under so much stress or traumatized because a loved one had just been found murdered. In fact, they're being accused of that murder. Whatever it is, they are vulnerable. They're in a stressful state of mind. And as a result of the process of interrogation, they are continuing to deny involvement. And then the detective doing the interrogating launches into a series of tactics that shocks most Americans to know is lawful. They start to lie to the suspect about the evidence. So, for example, Marty Tankliff, 17 years old, young 17 years old, wakes up one morning in 1990,
他们可能只有12、13、14、16岁。他们可能被剥夺了睡眠，从而变得容易受到操纵；他们可能承受着巨大的压力，或者因为挚爱刚刚被谋杀而受到创伤。事实上，他们正被指控犯下了那起谋杀案。无论是什么原因，他们都很脆弱。他们处于精神压力巨大的状态中。由于审讯过程，他们继续否认涉案。接着，负责审讯的警探开始实施一系列让大多数美国人感到震惊（并意识到这是合法的）的战术。他们开始就证据对嫌疑人撒谎。例如，马蒂·唐克利夫（Marty Tankleff），17岁，一个还很年轻的17岁少年，在1990年的一天早晨醒来，

14
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:51.000
and he finds lights are on all over the house. He finds his parents have been bludgeoned. His mother is dead in a pool of blood. He runs to his father's study and sees him gurgling, still alive but unconscious, also surrounded by blood. He calls the police, the police come, they identify him for no good reason as the suspect. And while his family is off at the emergency room in a hospital with his father, and he wants to be there and keep saying, I'd like to be with my family, not until we're done here, not until we're done here. So he's in an agitated, traumatized state. the detective starts to launch into a series of lies about the evidence.
他发现全屋的灯都亮着。他发现自己的父母遭到了重击殴打。他的母亲倒在血泊中死亡。他跑进父亲的书房，看到父亲在咯咯作响，虽然还活着但已失去意识，周围也全是血。他报警了。警察来了，在没有充分理由的情况下将他确定为嫌疑人。当时他的家人正和他的父亲一起前往医院的急诊室，而他希望去那里并一直说“我想和我的家人在一起”，警方却说“不行，等我们这里搞定再说，等我们这里搞定再说”。因此，他处于一种激动、受创的状态中，

15
00:08:51.000 --> 00:09:14.000
He says, Marty, you said you had nothing to do with this. How does that explain the fact that your hair was found in your mother's grasp? And Marty said, well, and that wasn't true. Marty said, well, I don't know how that could be possible. And then he asked, it looks like you use the shower this morning. And Marty said, no, I didn't. Because they couldn't explain the fact that there are two bloody murder scenes in this house. And Marty's clean.
警探开始就证据抛出一系列谎言。他说：“马蒂，你说你跟这事没关。那怎么解释在你母亲手中发现了你的头发这一事实呢？”马蒂说：“嗯……”而那其实并不是真的。马蒂说：“嗯，我不知道这怎么可能。”接着警探问：“看起来你今天早上冲过澡了。”马蒂说：“没有，我没有。”因为他们无法解释这栋房子里有两个血腥的谋杀现场，而马蒂身上却很干净的事实。

16
00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:46.000
They arrive at the scene and he's clean. They convinced him that they did a humidity test on the shower in his bedroom and that it proved that the shower had been used that morning. That was a lie. So now Marty's starting to wonder what the truth is and he's getting confused and disoriented. Then the detective gets up and physically leaves the room and leaves Marty in there with his partner. He comes back in, he says, Marty, I've got good news and bad news. The good news is I just contacted the hospital. your father has regained consciousness.
他们赶到现场时，他身上是干净的。他们说服了他，说他们对他的卧室淋浴间进行了湿度测试，证明那天早上淋浴间被使用过。那是个谎言。于是马蒂开始怀疑什么是真理，并变得困惑和迷失方向。接着，警探起身离开了房间，把马蒂留给他的搭档。他回来后说：“马蒂，我有个好消息和一个坏消息。好消息是，我刚联系了医院，你父亲已经恢复意识了。”

17
00:09:46.000 --> 00:10:05.000
The bad news is he said you did this. Oh my gosh. Wow. And that was a lie. And Marty said, my father never lies. If he said I did this, then I must have done this. That is the pattern that you see. Vulnerable suspect, dispositionally or rendered vulnerable, lied to about the evidence,
“坏消息是，他说这事是你干的。”“哦，天哪。哇。”而那是个谎言。马蒂说：“我父亲从不撒谎。如果他说是我干的，那一定是我干的。”这就是你所看到的模式：脆弱的嫌疑人（生性脆弱或被诱导变得脆弱），被欺骗了证据，

18
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:51.000
starting to get confused about reality. In some cases, the degree of internalization ends with they no longer can attest to their own innocence. They're confused. In some cases, it becomes a full-blown belief that they committed this crime and a search for the details as to how they did it. And so what happens is they eventually confess by using words like, I guess I did it, I guess I must have done it. It looks like I could always that kind of tentative, fragmentary language. And then ultimately they produce a fuller confession filled with the kinds of details that were handed to them through the process of interrogation. So if you're an observer in court and all you're hearing is that final statement,
开始对现实产生困惑。在某些情况下，内化程度的结局是他们再也无法证明自己的清白。他们糊涂了。在某些情况下，这变成了一种根深蒂固的信念，即他们犯下了这起罪行，并开始寻找他们是如何犯下该罪行的细节。因此发生的情况是，他们最终使用了诸如“我想是我干的”、“我想我一定干过”、“看起来我好像总是……”这样试探性的、碎片化的语言来认罪。最后，他们炮制出一份更加完整的供词，里面充斥着通过审讯过程灌输给他们的各种细节。因此，如果你是法庭上的旁听者，而你所听到的全部只有那份最终的陈述，

19
00:10:51.000 --> 00:11:29.000
there's no way you can get past it. And so that third category of false confessions, fascinating, you see it over and over again, again, not nearly as prevalent as the compliant false confessions, but it's a third storyline where the process of interrogation actually converts the beliefs and memories, and sometimes they actually confabulate memories of what they did and how they did it. What is the most common thing that you see in these interrogations that leads you to believe someone may not be telling the truth or is feeling intimidated? I think you've touched a little bit on this already, but I'm curious in some more examples.
你是绝对无法看清真相的。因此，这第三类虚假口供——令人着迷，你会一次又一次地看到它——虽然远不如顺从型虚假口供那么普遍，但它是第三种剧情：审讯过程实际上改变了他们的信念和记忆，有时他们甚至会虚构出关于自己做了什么以及如何做的记忆。在这些审讯中，你看到的最常见的迹象是什么，让你相信某人可能在说谎或者感到受到了恐吓？我想你之前已经稍微触及过这一点了，但我对更多的例子很感兴趣。

20
00:11:29.000 --> 00:12:08.000
How are law enforcement officials contributing to these false confessions? They are contributing mightily to false confessions. And there's two things I should say about this. First, you imagine, imagine yourself sitting in a courtroom watching a videotaped confession. So the suspect has been brought to the point of giving a statement. And at that point, the videotape recorder is turned on and a statement is given, and that statement later is played in court. I can tell you as an empirical matter, we've done these studies. And certainly the wrongful convictions experiences of people who have been wrongfully convicted will attest,
执法人员是如何导致这些虚假口供的？他们对虚假口供有着巨大的推波助澜作用。对此我有两点要说。首先，你可以想象一下自己坐在法庭上观看录像供词的场景。嫌疑人已经被逼到了招供的地步。就在那时，录像机被打开，供词被给出，而这份供词随后在法庭上被播放。我可以从实证的角度告诉你，我们做过这些研究。当然，那些被错误定罪的人的经历也足以证明，

21
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:57.000
judges, juries, laypeople, they can't tell the difference between a true confession and a false confession just by watching the confession. I went into a prison outside of Boston with colleagues. We had prisoners on tape confessing to the crimes for which they were incarcerated, and then we asked each one to make up a confession to a crime he did not commit. People cannot tell the difference between those two sets of confessions. And the story of every false confession is not a story of what happened in the police interrogation room. It's a story of the fact that once that false confession was taken and recanted and the person pled not guilty and went to trial, in every instance, a prosecutor, a judge, a jury, and typically an appeals court, believed that
法官、陪审团、外行仅仅通过观看认罪过程，根本无法分辨真假供词的区别。我曾与同事一起进入波士顿郊外的一座监狱。我们让囚犯在录像中承认他们因其服刑的罪行，然后我们要求每个人编造一份他并未犯下的罪行的供词。人们根本无法分辨这两组供词的区别。每一个虚假口供的故事都不是关于警察审讯室内发生了什么的故事，而是关于这样一个事实：一旦这份虚假口供被录取、随后又被翻供，且当事人做出了无罪抗辩并走上审判庭时，在每一种情况下，检察官、法官、陪审团，通常还有上诉法院，都会相信那份

22
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:39.000
false confession. The second terrible subplot in these cases is not just that the false confession was taken. It's that all the safety nets we believe are built into the system fail. People believe false confessions. So first thing it's important to know is there's no way to identify what it is that law enforcement does simply by watching, that contributes simply by watching the final statement. That's why what's most important and whenever I clamor for reform and other psychologists in this area clamor for reform, the number one rule for reform is record the entire process. The minute they walk in to the road.
