00:26:02.000 --> 00:27:00.000
has either we actually vary whether his demeanor is hostile and close-minded, or whether he seems open and willing to. to hear their story. They are all incentivized to make themselves appear innocent. And the first thing he does is read them their rights and look to see whether they're willing to sign the waiver. And by a large margin of 81% to 36%, those who had nothing to hide, those whose accounts of their whereabouts and what they did would be truthful, 81% of them waive their rights compared to only 36% of them who actually had committed the mock crime. And they told us exactly what the wrongfully convicted say. Well, I didn't do anything wrong. He'll see that. Two explanations I had for what was happening here, and they're kind of related. One is people have a belief in a just world.
侦探的表现要么是敌对和封闭的，要么看起来是开放且愿意倾听他们的故事的。他们所有人都被激励着让自己看起来是清白的。他做的第一件事就是宣读他们的权利，并查看他们是否愿意签署放弃书。结果显示，以81%对36%的巨大差距，那些没有什么可隐瞒的人、那些关于他们的行踪和所作所为的陈述是真实的人中，有81%的人放弃了权利，而真正犯下模拟罪行的人中只有36%的人放弃了权利。他们告诉我们的完全是那些被冤枉的人所说的话：“嗯，我没做错什么，他会看出来的。”我对这里发生的事情有两个解释，而且它们有点相关。一是人们对公正世界的信仰。

43
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:45.000
Now, we know that. There is research and psychology on the belief in a just world. People tend to believe that the world is generally a just place. Good things happen to good people. Bad things happen to bad people. The fact of the matter is, if I did nothing wrong, that will come out. Justice will figure that out. And the second interesting phenomenon in psychology that I think is informative is what is called the illusion of transparency. People believe that their truths telling and their lies are transparent. People believe that when they utter the truth, others will believe them, and that when they lie, others will figure that out. And that illusion of transparency, gives comfort to the person who did nothing wrong. So when you hear the wrongfully convicted talk
现在，我们知道了这一点。心理学中有关于对公正世界信仰的研究。人们倾向于认为世界总体上是一个公正的地方。好人有好报，坏人有恶报。事实是，如果我没有做错任何事，真相就会大白，正义会弄清楚的。我认为具有启发意义的心理学中的第二个有趣现象被称为“透明度错觉”。人们相信他们的说实话和说谎是透明的。人们相信当他们说真话时别人会相信他们，而当他们撒谎时别人会看出来。这种透明度错觉给那些没有做错事的人带来了安慰。因此，当你听到被冤枉的人谈论

44
00:27:45.000 --> 00:28:33.000
about why did you waive your rights, and in fact, in some cases they explain the reason I gave in and confessed was I figured, you know what, they'll proceed with their investigation, and in the end they'll see I did nothing wrong. Now, this gets to, I think, a very important point that I did not see coming until I had conversations with some of these false confessors. And it is a concept that I have gone on to call the phenomenology of innocence. An innocent person, you would think that the thing that protects an innocent person is his or her knowledge of his innocence, right? I'll be damned if I'm going to confess to something I know I didn't do. However, innocent people sometimes confess after hours of interrogation because they're led to believe that there is additional evidence
他们为什么要放弃权利时，事实上，在某些情况下他们解释说，我之所以妥协并招供，是因为我想，你知道吗，他们会继续调查，最终他们会看到我没有做错任何事。现在，这触及了我认为非常重要的一点，在我与其中一些虚假供述者交谈之前，我没有预料到这一点。这是一个我后来称之为“清白现象学”的概念。对于一个无辜的人，你可能会认为保护无辜者的是他或她对自己清白的了解，对吧？我绝不会承认一件我知道我没有做过的事情。然而，无辜的人有时在经过数小时的审讯后会招供，因为他们被引导相信有更多的证据

45
00:28:33.000 --> 00:29:11.000
coming. And when they're led to believe that there's additional evidence coming, that belief, in becomes a promise of future exoneration, which paradoxly makes it easier to confess. So a number of innocent people have confessed to get out of a bad situation, believing when the police do the rest of their investigation, they'll see this wasn't me. So in a funny way, innocence can be your own enemy in that situation. And that is the same phenomenon, the same explanation as to why innocent people waive their rights. They'll look you're right in the eye and say, well, I didn't need a lawyer. I didn't do anything wrong.
即将出现。当他们被引导相信有更多的证据即将到来时，这种信念就变成了未来洗清罪名的承诺，这反而使招供变得更容易了。因此，许多无辜的人为了摆脱糟糕的处境而招供，他们相信当警察完成其余的调查时，他们会发现这不是我干的。所以从某种滑稽的角度来看，在这种情况下，清白会成为你自己的敌人。这就是为什么无辜的人会放弃他们的权利的同一种现象、同一种解释。他们会直视你的眼睛说：“嗯，我不需要律师，我又没做错什么。”

46
00:29:11.000 --> 00:30:00.000
I think it's interesting that you're studying this and you were telling me this before we actually started recording, but you think it's important for a psychologist to be out there and making people aware of this kind of information. It's very important. You know, the psychology underlying false confessions is basic. We have a wealth of studies that we've done in laboratories and field settings, surveys and whatnot from the 1980s forward. All of it has been summarized in a white paper that APA Division 41 has produced. All of it is summarized in now seven amicus briefs that the APA has submitted to various state courts. But in a sense, we almost didn't need that literature to understand the underlying psychology.
我觉得很有趣的是你在研究这个，你实际上在我们开始录音之前就告诉我了，你认为心理学家走出去让人们了解这类信息是很重要的。这非常重要。你知道，虚假供述背后的心理学是基础性的。我们有大量自20世纪80年代以来在实验室和实地环境中进行的研究、调查等成果。所有这些都总结在APA（美国心理学协会）第41分部制作的一份白皮书中。所有这些都总结在APA向各州法院提交的七份法庭之友简报中。但在某种程度上，我们几乎不需要这些文献来理解背后的心理学。

47
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:45.000
It is so basic. If you understand the principles of reinforcement, if you understand the way Skinner used to shape rats in a Skinner, box, if you understand the reward contingencies that affect people's decision-making, if you remember back to Milgram's obedience experiments where people were under pressure step by step increasingly to violate their conscience, all of that is directly relevant to what happens in an interrogation room. And so psychologists, I think, are uniquely poised to help in these matters. And the reason I think raising public awareness is important. I have spent, and others have spent a lot of years trying to reform the system from the top
这非常基础。如果你理解强化原则，如果你理解斯金纳过去如何在斯金纳箱里塑造老鼠，如果你理解影响人们决策的奖赏相依性，如果你回想起米尔格拉姆的服从实验，当时人们在压力下一步步地违背自己的良心，所有这些都与审讯室里发生的事情直接相关。因此，我认为心理学家在协助处理这些事情方面具有独特的优势。我认为提高公众意识很重要的原因在于：我和其他人花了很多年试图从上到下改革这个体系

48
00:30:45.000 --> 00:31:20.000
down, trying to, we speak to state legislators, we speak to state innocence commissions, we talk to groups of judges, we try to get the laws changed from the top down. And that has been somewhat successful. But what has become apparent to me, and it's become apparent in some very high-profile cases, like the Central Park 5, like Brendan Dassey's situation. What has become apparent is that another way to manage this, another way to exert our influence and, I think, help to reform a broken system,
试图与州议员对话，与州冤案委员会对话，与法官群体对话，试图自上而下地改变法律。这在一定程度上取得了成功。但对我来说显而易见的是，并且在一些备受瞩目的案件中也变得显而易见，比如中央公园五人组案，比如布伦丹·达西的境况。显而易见的是，解决这个问题的另一种方法，施加我们影响力的另一种方法，以及我认为有助于改革一个破碎体系的方法，

49
00:31:20.000 --> 00:32:07.000
is to get involved in raising public awareness. I think, again, the making a murderer documentary tells me a lot. Here's a case where 16-year-old Brendan Dassey, 10 years ago, gives a confession. Now, mind you, the jury got to see the tape or parts of the tape of that interrogation, but they didn't get to hear from an expert explaining that tape. And so he gets convicted. Appeals within the state are exhausted. Everything looks like he's going to spend the next 25 years to life in prison. Then in December of 2015, the documentary is aired. There is a public outcry. 20 million people watched the documentary in the first month or so. and there is outrage over the treatment of Brendan Dassey and his confession.
就是参与提高公众意识。我认为，“制造杀人犯”这部纪录片再次说明了很多问题。这是一个10年前16岁的布伦丹·达西做出虚假供述的案件。现在请注意，陪审团看到了那次审讯的录像带或部分录像带，但他们没有听到专家的解释。因此他被定罪了。州内的上诉已经用尽。一切迹象表明他将在监狱里度过接下来的25年至无期徒刑。然后，在2015年12月，这部纪录片播出了。公众哗然。在头一个月左右的时间里，有2000万人观看了这部纪录片，人们对布伦丹·达西的遭遇及其供述感到愤怒。

50
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:52.000
Within six months, a federal judge overturned his conviction, arguing that the confession was coerced. That judge had his ruling appealed, and then a three-judge federal panel affirmed that ruling. Now, Dassey, at the moment we are speaking right now, is still incarcerated in Wisconsin because the prosecutor's office has appealed those federal decisions and wants to go back and retry him and has argued that he should remain incarcerated to that point. But the fact of the matter is DASI wasn't going anywhere until public awareness was raised. That is another way in which we can make important change. And I don't know that there's another profession as uniquely poised as psychology.
在六个月内，一名联邦法官推翻了他的定罪，认为该供述是受到胁迫的。该法官的裁决遭到上诉，随后由一个三名法官组成的联邦合议庭维持了该裁决。目前，在我们谈话的当下，达西仍然被关押在威斯康星州，因为检方对这些联邦裁决提出了上诉，并希望回去重新审判他，并主张他在那之前应该继续被关押。但事实是，如果公众意识没有被唤醒，达西哪儿也去不了。这是我们能够带来重要改变的另一种方式。而且我不知道还有哪个职业能像心理学这样具有独特的优势。

51
00:32:52.000 --> 00:33:15.000
And you know what? If we don't step in to inform the public about what's happening, somebody else will. Phil Vett Void, who is less expert and less informed. Well, Dr. Kasten, thank you so much for being here today. It's been really interesting here. Pleasure. Thanks for listening. If you would like more information on the topics we discussed
你知道吗？如果我们不介入向公众通报正在发生的事情，其他人就会这么做。菲尔·韦特·沃伊德，他就不那么专业，也不那么知情。好的，卡斯滕博士，非常感谢您今天来到这里。听您分享真的非常有趣。我的荣幸。感谢收听。如果您想了解更多关于我们讨论的话题的信息

